56: 11 The court of appeals further explained that “[e]ven assuming” that the statute is ambiguous, prior Executive Branch practice “strongly counsels against adopting” the government’s interpretation of Section 1225(b)(2)(A). App., infra, 50a; see id. at 50a-55a. Finally, the court reasoned, were the statute ambiguous, constitutional-avoidance principles would support respondent given the court’s “grave” concern that applying Section 1225(b)(2)(A) to aliens in respondent’s shoes would violate due process. Id. at 55a; see id. at 55a-59a. Judge Cabranes concurred separately “to emphasize key points.” App., infra, 61a; see id. at 61a-68a. REASONS FOR GRANTING THE PETITION This case presents the question whether 8 U.S.C. 1225(b)(2)(A) mandates the detention of aliens like respondent who are present in the United States without having been admitted. The government recently filed a petition for a writ of certiorari in another case involving the same question, Raycraft v. Lopez-Campos, petition for cert. pending, No. 25-1415 (filed June 22, 2026). This Court should hold this petition pending the disposition of the petition in Lopez-Campos and then dispose of this petition as appropriate. As the government explained in Lopez-Campos, the courts of appeals are divided as to the lawfulness of applying Section 1225(b)(2)(A) to aliens present in the country without admission, such as after an illegal entry. The Fifth and Eighth Circuits have correctly held that Section 1225(b)(2)(A)’s plain language covers such aliens and mandates their detention pending removal proceedings. Buenrostro-Mendez v. Bondi, 166 F.4th 494 (5th Cir. 2026), petition for cert. pending, No. 26-43 (filed July 2, 2026); Avila v. Bondi, 170 F.4th 1128 (8th Cir. 2026). The Second Circuit below, along with the
