| CBS’s “tens of thousands” count is absent from the five records the page names | The page correctly limits its absence claim to those records | Confirmed within that corpus | **Moderate:** the [Fourth Circuit](https://www.ca4.uscourts.gov/opinions/257044.P.pdf) reports over twenty thousand *cases*. That does not verify CBS’s count of immigrants detained without hearings. Neither figure should be substituted for the other. |

## 7. Source and Corroboration Problems

- **Moderate, grade B: The rehearing pin is only the order.** The page says, “Judges Bianco and Nathan, joined by four others, concurred in that denial. Judge Menashi dissented.” The pinned [three-page order](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-detention/data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-rehearing-2026-09-25.txt:109) names additional opinions; the [full court document](https://ww3.ca2.uscourts.gov/decisions/OPN/25-3141_complete%20en%20banc_opn.pdf) includes Judge Schwartz’s statement, “Although I agree with the analysis in the dissent, I support the decision to deny rehearing en banc.” The correct account is that denial of full-court review was not unanimous agreement with the panel’s reasoning.

- **Moderate, grade B: An available underlying filing is absent from the records list.** The page says the docket required the petition and response to be submitted “in paper form only.” The [DOJ copy of the petition](https://www.justice.gov/d9/2026-07/barbosa_da_cunha_hold_pet_-_final.pdf) includes the district court decision in its appendix and explains why the government preferred a different Supreme Court case. The correct account distinguishes a docket without an electronic petition link from a petition unavailable to readers.

- The page’s AP, CBS and Hill reports do not provide three independent evidentiary lineages for the grant. Their common underlying event is the [Supreme Court order](https://www.supremecourt.gov/orders/courtorders/100126zr_6j37.pdf). The court opinions, agency guidance, 1997 rule and individual filings are separate records for the broader story.

- The manifest lists **no video pins or transcripts** for this event. There were therefore no pinned video frames to examine at one frame per second.

## 8. Reconstructed Story

**Established:** In July 2025, DHS adopted a new reading that places people present without admission under mandatory-detention authority rather than the bond process previously used for many people arrested in the interior. The Board adopted that position. Barbosa da Cunha, arrested years after entry, challenged the denial of a bond hearing; a district court ordered a hearing or release, an immigration judge released him on bond, and the Second Circuit affirmed the statutory ruling. Other circuits split. On October 1, 2026, the Supreme Court granted review of whether § 1225(b)(2)(A) requires detention of people like him. [ICE guidance](https://iptp-production.s3.amazonaws.com/media/documents/2025.07.08_ICE_-_Interim_Guidance_Regarding_Detention_Authority_for_Applicants_0JGK0CH.pdf), [Second Circuit opinion](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-detention/data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt:155), [order](https://www.supremecourt.gov/orders/courtorders/100126zr_6j37.pdf).

**Disputed:** Whether the statute’s definition of an applicant for admission makes a person living inside the country “seeking admission” when later arrested. The [Eighth Circuit](https://ecf.ca8.uscourts.gov/opndir/26/03/253248P.pdf) says yes; the [Second](https://www.justice.gov/d9/2026-07/barbosa_da_cunha_hold_pet_-_final.pdf) and [Fourth](https://www.ca4.uscourts.gov/opinions/257044.P.pdf) say no for people in the circumstances before them. The separate due process question remains open in this grant.

## 9. Remaining Unknowns

This audit remains bounded by three high-value checks: the full paper-filed **response and reply** in 26-104; a defensible dataset separating people held *because of this interpretation* from all ICE detainees and from habeas filings; and any later docket changes, briefing or argument schedule. The July 8 ICE memorandum was located and read, but its scanned copy has imperfect text extraction, so quotations from it should be checked visually before publication.

