## First read

The page told me that the Supreme Court took a dispute over whether people living in the country without admission can seek bond after the government changed its detention position.

I still wanted to know whether the related petitions had moved and whether anyone could establish how many people are held under this reading. I first lost the thread here: “By the grant the dispute had reached every numbered federal appeals court: on September 10 the Fourth Circuit wrote that the First, Second, Third, Sixth, Seventh, Ninth, Tenth, and Eleventh Circuits had concluded that the bond statute applies to noncitizens inside the country, like the petitioners in those appeals, who must be given a bond hearing, and that the Fifth and Eighth Circuits had ruled that the detention statute applies to all noncitizens who have not been admitted.”

## The story

The record supports this story: after a July 2025 change denied bond review to people arrested inside the country years after entry, Barbosa da Cunha obtained a hearing and release, appeals courts split over the governing statute, and the Supreme Court granted review of that statutory question on October 1, 2026. The [1997 rule](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-detention/data/sources/immigration-detention/fr-detention-rule-1997-03-06.txt) says this group “will be eligible for bond and bond redetermination”; the [July memorandum](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-detention/data/sources/immigration-detention/cbp-detention-memo-2025-07-10.txt) instead assigns “section 235 of the Immigration and Nationality Act (INA), rather than section 236”; the [order](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-detention/data/sources/immigration-detention/scotus-order-list-2026-10-01.txt) says “The petitions for writs of certiorari are granted.”

The page has the right angle and opening act. It states the changed practice and leaves the statutory ruling unresolved. One passage near the top now gives the dated order of the change, denied hearing, release, appellate ruling, and grant. Its arrest-to-release sentence is crowded, but the reader no longer has to assemble the sequence from separate sections.

The event has not moved in the checked primary dockets as of October 3. The [case docket](https://www.supremecourt.gov/docket/docketfiles/html/public/26-104.html) ends its proceedings with “Petition GRANTED.” and an October 1 filing instruction. The [Sixth Circuit petition](https://www.supremecourt.gov/docket/docketfiles/html/public/25-1415.html) and [Fifth Circuit petition](https://www.supremecourt.gov/docket/docketfiles/html/public/26-43.html) show no later disposition. I also checked the [ACLU’s October 1 statement](https://www.aclu.org/press-releases/aclu-comment-on-supreme-court-agreeing-to-hear-challenge-over-trump-mandatory-detention-policy), a primary actor statement reached through coverage; it adds no later event.

| Reader-model answer | Does the marked story paragraph carry it? |
| --- | --- |
| 1. What happened | Yes. It gives the date, grant, statutory question, and limit of the order. |
| 2. Why it matters | Yes. It gives his circumstances, the loss of bond under the government’s reading, and the wider split. The circuit sentence makes that last point harder to retain. |
| 3. Easiest wrong reading | Yes. The page distinguishes a grant from a ruling and a count of cases from a count of people held. |
| 4. Mechanism | Yes. It distinguishes being an applicant for admission from seeking admission and says a hearing does not itself order release. |
| 5. What changed | Yes. The dated paragraph covers the old rule, new position, Board ruling, appeal, rehearing denial, and grant. |
| 6. What remains unresolved | Yes. It identifies the unset argument day and separates this statutory grant from the due process question. |

## The top

The first story sentence says what happened. The headline survives the easiest wrong reading: “agreed to decide” does not claim a ruling or a population count. Barbosa da Cunha is introduced by full name. The dek uses “detention statute,” “bond statute,” and “parole” before the later explanation, but states the practical bond-versus-parole distinction. It passes a cold read.

## Line edits

Page bytes are from the [story view](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-detention/checks/audits/immigration-detention--2026-10-01-supreme-court-takes-up-bond-hearings-2026-10-03-r2-editor-page.txt).

1. **Minor, new. CUT:** “The bond statute is the statute under which the government may release someone.” The dek already gives that consequence, and the mechanism paragraph develops it.

2. **Minor, new. TIGHTEN:** “On September 26, 2025, officers arrested Barbosa da Cunha while he was driving to work in Norwood, Massachusetts; an immigration judge denied him a bond hearing, a district court ordered a bond hearing or release within ten days, and an immigration judge released him on bond after finding no danger and no flight risk.” Four consequential acts compete inside one sentence. Keep their order and dates.

3. **Minor, new. CUT:** “Petitioner may be an “applicant for admission,” but he is not “seeking admission.”” The preceding paragraph explains the distinction and cites the opinion; the display repeats it.

4. **Minor, residual. TIGHTEN:** “By the grant the dispute had reached every numbered federal appeals court: on September 10 the Fourth Circuit wrote that the First, Second, Third, Sixth, Seventh, Ninth, Tenth, and Eleventh Circuits had concluded that the bond statute applies to noncitizens inside the country, like the petitioners in those appeals, who must be given a bond hearing, and that the Fifth and Eighth Circuits had ruled that the detention statute applies to all noncitizens who have not been admitted.” The [author’s ledger](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-detention/checks/working-notes/immigration-detention--2026-10-01-supreme-court-takes-up-bond-hearings.md) records the added Fourth Circuit passage after the earlier gap. The sides now appear, but this single sentence obscures the result.

5. **Minor, new. CLARIFY:** “Two related petitions, one from the Fifth Circuit in Buenrostro-Mendez and one from the Sixth Circuit in Lopez-Campos, have not been granted.” Anchor this changeable docket status to October 3; the current [Fifth](https://www.supremecourt.gov/docket/docketfiles/html/public/26-43.html) and [Sixth](https://www.supremecourt.gov/docket/docketfiles/html/public/25-1415.html) docket pages still list no grant.

## Holes

No new answer-changing hole requires added copy. The two questions left by my first read are stated as unknowns on the page:

1. **How many people are held under this reading?** Unknown. The [Fourth Circuit](https://www.ca4.uscourts.gov/opinions/257044.P.pdf) says “sided with the petitioners in over twenty thousand individual cases”; that is a case count. The page says no court or agency record supplies the detention count. This bears on answer 3 and displaces nothing.

2. **When will this case be argued?** Unknown. The [October 1 argument list](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-detention/data/sources/immigration-detention/scotus-granted-noted-2026-10-01.txt) prints “RHONEY V. BARBOSA DA CUNHA” and “Granted: 10/1/26” without an argument day on that line. The page states the limit. This bears on answer 6 and displaces nothing.

## The budget

About **1,100 words now**. The two cuts remove about **30 words**; tightening can save roughly **10 to 20** more. Holes add **zero**. Target: about **1,050 words**.

## Decision

The six marked answers are present, the opening sequence is intact, and the current primary dockets do not put the piece behind its event. The numbered edits are copy work, with no Critical or Major item.

VERDICT: SHIP
